<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frank W. Carpenter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Frank Watson Carpenter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frank W. Carpenter was an American colonial official in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> who served as the first civilian governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and Sulu from 1914 to 1920, after the military-run Moro Province was abolished. He pursued a policy of integrating the Muslim south into the Philippine state through schools, roads and Filipino officials, and in 1915 negotiated the agreement by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Sulu gave up his temporal powers.</p></div>
